      {
        "day_number": 7,
        "title": "The Praise of a Godly Woman (Part 4) (1)",
        "content": "<p>—Coniun quæ adulterinum animū gerit, etiam si timòre viri non adulterium perpetrat: tamen quod deest aperi, inest voluntati, Casta verò alitèr timet: nam &amp; ipsa times virum; sed castè. Deniq, timet illa, no vir insestus adueniat, ista ne offensus abscedat. S. Aug. epist. 120. c. 21. to. 2.</p><p>Ruben violat Balamdum nimium grandis effectus est, quia timor si simius est, dum se custodire nititur, format inutiles imaginationes, quibus se connoluens à salutaribus impeditur. Tract. 10. super, Magnificat. part 3.</p><p>Lege Esaiam: vide quantia subiecerit timorem vt faceret irreprehensibilam &amp; bonum Timorem. Spiritus inquit Sapientia &amp;c. Talle Timori Domini ista &amp; est irrationabilis &amp; insipiens Timor, vnus ex illis: Foris pugnæ, intus Timores. In Psal. 118. Ser. 5. tom. 4.</p><p>Lastly, it is an Euerlasting Feare, ever encreasing , and Enduring forever , though not in respect of the Act of Declination or eschewing of sinne , because in Heauen there is no feare of sinning ; yet in respect of the Act of Celebration or Reuerencing God, because there the Saints doe nothing else; but still Give Glory to Him, and Worship Him with humble Acknowledgement of their owne Vnworthinesse, Psal. 19. 9. with Reuel. 4. 10. 11.</p><p>Now (Honourable and Beloued) though I have set nothing at all besides this Heauenly Manna before your eyes; yet your full Soules must not Loath it. For if that only is to be praised which is excellent , then (by your leaue) I must stand somewhat longer upon the Excellencie of this Feare , before a Woman can be praised that has it.</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Psal. 115. 11. &amp; 147. 11. Esai. 50. 10.</p><p>Psal. 19. 9</p><p>2 Cor. 7. 1.</p><p>Hebr. 12. 28.</p><p>Eccles. 2. 17. &amp; 21. 6.</p><p>Esai. 66. 2. Eccle. 2. 15.</p><p>Iob 1. 1.</p><p>Act. 10. 35, 2, 31. Eccles. 25. 1.</p><p>Psal. 112. 1. &amp; 128. 1.</p><p>Psal. 135. 13. &amp; 118. 4 &amp; 22. 23.</p><p>1 King. 18. 3. 12. Tob. 14. 4.</p><p>Psal. 19. 9. Reuel. 7. 10, 11 &amp; 19. 1, 3, 4, 5, 6.</p><p>Bonau. lib. 3. d. 34. q. 3. p. 89. Tho. Aquin. II q. 79. a. II</p><p>In cœlook, vbi non est peccatum Gloria est &amp; perpetua laus &amp; indefessæ præconia. S. Ierom. ad Therdoram. Epitaph. Lucinij. Timorem Offensa &amp; Timorem Pœnæ. Gloria Timorem Patriæ propter coram imperfectionem nō paritur. In tuto enim erimus et ȃ pœnæ et ab offensa. Paris. de Virtutibus, lit. A. F.</p><p>Laudare plus est quàm probare &amp; prædicare. Nam Laudamus id quod excellit &amp;c. Auso. Popma de Differen. Virt. l. 3.</p><p>I demand then what doe you count Excellent? Riches, Honour, Life. Why, these are neuer well gotten, nor well kept; but by the Feare of the Lord . So says Salomon , By the feare of the Lord are Riches and Honour, and Life . Say what you will, it must be an Excellent thing wherwith Christ Jesus Himselfe was Filled, and that was with The Spirit of this Feare, Esai. 11. 2. . An excellent thing which God Himselfe so earnestly desires to be still in vs, and that is this Feare. O (says He) that there were such an Heart in them, that they would feare me, and keepe my Commandements alwaies , which is indeed the whole Dutie of Man ; without which (Saint Bernard concludes) Every man is Nothing . He is not a Man (reasons a Schoole-man ) but the Shadow of a Man; because He imployes not his Soule to that noble End for which he had it, namely, to be squared and ruled by the feare of the Lord : without which no man can so much as Begin to be wise, because this Feare is the Beginning of wisdome , nor so much as Begin to Love God , because this Feare is the Beginning of the Love of God . It is the Salt (alludes Blesensis ) that must be in every Sacrifice , in every Worke we doe , so that there is no Seruing God , no Reioycing in Him , no heartie Repenting , no Chast Conuersing , no Perfecting Holinesse , no Working out our Saluation , but with Feare and Trembling ; no there is no Saluation , no Blessednesse without Continuing in this Feare , Prou. 28. 14. Againe, is not that Excellent, that will make vs more Excellent than our Neighbours , that will Exalt vs aboue them, that will keepe our hearts from Hardning , our Houses from Ouerthrowing ? but nothing can doe this; but this Feare of the Lord . This feare (says Paris. ) can cause a spiritual Earth-quake in a mans Heart, able to ouerthrow all the Deuils strongest holds, any Bosome-sinne, be it neuer so pleasing and profitable, by reason of that Contrarietie and Opposition that is betweene Lying in any Sweet Sinne, and Liuing in Gods Feare and Fauour, as you may see, Leuit. 25. 36.</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Prou. 22. 4. &amp; 19. 23. Eccles. 1. 11, 12. &amp; 23. 27. &amp; 40. 26, 27. &amp; 10. 20, 22.</p><p>Inter Laudes meas &amp; illa est eximia: quod ipsum Christum Dominū Apothecam, immo fontem Gratiarum omnium &amp; Virtutum replere dictus sum &amp;c. Paris. de Moribus, fol. 99. Lit. P.</p><p>Deut. 5. 29.</p><p>Eccles. 12. 13. Deum time—Ergo si hoc est omnis Homo, absq, hoc Nihil omnis Homo. Serm. 20. in Cant.</p><p>Vsus enim humani animi pendet à Timore conspictus Diuini tanquam à primi regula. Caiet. in Eccles. c. 8 13. v.</p><p>Prou. 9. 10. Iob 28. 28.</p><p>Eccles. 25. 12.</p><p>Leuit. 2. 13. Pet. Bles. ser. 36. p. 430.</p><p>Psal. 2. 11.</p><p>2 Cor. 7. 11. Eccl. 21. 6.</p><p>1 Pet. 3. 2.</p><p>2 Cor. 7. 1.</p><p>Philip. 2. 12.</p><p>Eccles. 2. 10. 1 Tim. 2. 15.</p>",
