      {
        "day_number": 10,
        "title": "The Praise of a Godly Woman (Part 5) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Gen. 23. 2. Ioh. 11. 33, 35. 1 King. 14. 13. 1 Thes. 4. 13. Eccles. 22. 11, 12 &amp; 38. 16, 17.</p><p>Act. 8. 2. 2 Chron. 32. 33.</p><p>Iam. 5. 10. Hebr. 12. 1. &amp; 13. 7. 1 Cor. 11. 1.</p><p>1 Sam. 2. 30. Deut. 26. 19. Ioh. 12. 26. Esai. 8. 13. Timor Hominis, Dei Honor est. Tert. de Pœnit. c. 7</p><p>Tho. Aquin. 22. q. 103. a. 1. ad 3.</p><p>Psal. 12. 6.</p><p>Psal. 16. 3.</p><p>Psal. 83. 3.</p><p>Mal. 3. 17.</p><p>2 Cor. 6. 18,</p><p>Cant. 4. 9.</p><p>Matth. 12. 50.</p><p>Hos. 2. 19.</p><p>Cant. 2. 10.</p><p>Cant. 2. 14.</p><p>Cant. 2. 13.</p><p>Cant. 6. 10.</p><p>Prou. 5. 19.</p><p>Ezek. 24. 16. Eccles. 36. 22.</p><p>Gen. 2. 18. Eccles. 36. 24.</p><p>Mal. 2. 14.</p><p>Eccles. 36. 24.</p><p>Prou. 31. 11.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 3, 23.</p><p>Prou. 18. 22.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 14.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 15.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 1.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 13.</p><p>Prou. 31. 23.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 22.</p><p>Prou. 19. 14.</p><p>Eccles. 40. 19.</p><p>Prou. 12. 4. Non annulus, non torques aureus, non monile, sed Corona. Cartw. in Prou.</p><p>Psal. 21. 3. Esth. 2. 17</p><p>Eccles. 25. 10.</p><p>Eccles. 10. 24.</p><p>Eccles. 7. 19.</p><p>Prou. 31. 10.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 14. 15.</p><p>To Her Children she shall be Commended; because by her they have a place of Refuge ; by Her they have good meanes to bring and continue true Honour upon them , and if They (the Fruit) be a great Blessing (as it is to have Issue by such a One) what is the Root that bears it ?</p><p>But I must hasten: How shall she be Praised in respect of her Parents? even as Rachel for doing seruice to them as to her Masters (the true propertie of one that fears the Lord .) In respect of her kindred by mariage, as Ruth , louing them , Dealing kindly with them , and Cleauing to them . And in respect of her kindred by bloud, as Esther , who did the Commandement of Mordecay when she was a Queene, like as when she was brought vp with Him , who was exceedingly grieued at his Griefe , and procured the Enlargement and Deliuerance of her kindred with her Feasts , her Teares , and the Hazard of her Life .</p><p>In regard of her Seruants also, she shall be commended because she Builds her House : She is like the Merchants ship, She fetches her food from farre , She gives food to her Houshold , She clothes them all with Scarlet , and She looks so well to their waies , that As the Sunne when it arises in the high Heauen; So is her beautie in the Ordering of her House .</p><p>Lastly, because all the Essentiall Glory and Fairenesse, which is to bee found in the whole Church, The Woman cloathed with the Sunne , as that of Iustification &amp; Sanctification &amp;c. belongs to every Member of the Church , and cōsequently to every Woman fearing the Lord , therefore to Gods people she shall be commended, as one of the Hands of the Church dropping with sweet smelling Mirrhe : as the Curtaines of Salomon ; As a Lilly among thornes ; A Garden inclosed: A Spring shut vp. A Fountain sealed .</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Eccles. 26. 1.</p><p>Eccles. 36. 23. Sine Muliere igitur Homo non habet Laudem, in Muliere prædicatur, &amp;c. S. Ambros. Instit. Virg. cap. 3. tom. 1.</p><p>Prou. 14. 26. &amp; 11. 22.</p><p>Deut. 5. 29. Psal. 112. 2.</p><p>Psal. 127. 3.</p><p>Psal. 128. 3.</p><p>Gen. 29. 9.</p><p>Eccles. 3. 7.</p><p>Ruth. 4. 15.</p><p>Ruth. 1. 8.</p><p>Ruth. 1. 14. &amp; c. 2. 11</p><p>Esth. 2. 20.</p><p>Esth. 4. 4.</p><p>Esth. 4. 16.</p><p>Esth. 8. 3.</p><p>Esth. 4. 11, 16.</p><p>Prou. 14. 1.</p><p>Prou. 31. 14.</p><p>Prou. 31. 15.</p><p>Prou. 31. 21.</p><p>Prou. 31. 27.</p><p>Eccles. 26. 16.</p><p>Reuel. 12. 1.</p><p>But when shall she have all this Praise and of whom? Not by and by, nor of Every one , for Praise is not comely in the mouth of every one, of every scoffing Ishmael .</p><p>But first of the Time.</p><p>Many when they heare a Promise (and a Promise I told you at first, this is) thinke to have it by and by; but they marke not, that a Promise and the Fruition of it is not all at once. It must be waited for , especially this Promise of Praise: Until the Lord come (as the Apostles limit the Time) and then , at his Appearing, she shall have praise of God . Then at his Appearing , Her Faith shall be found to Praise and Honour, and Glory . Therefore in the mean while, best for her to Feare the Lord , and so be praise-worthy, than to be praised for the present.</p><p>1. Not only because it is safest praising Her as a Master of a ship is (says S. Ambrose ) when she is safely arriued in the Hauen, past all danger of shipwracke: or growing more proud by her praise (which many Liuing doe) as Herod for one , and that Philosopher for another, whose soule being before no bigger than a mans finger, became so puft vp and swolne with others commending him, that (as Arrianus reports) it grew greater than two Cubits .</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Gal. 3. 28. Ephes. 4. 15.16. Cant. 2. 10.—Cum ipsos cogitatis amantes, non virum &amp; Fœminam, sed verbum &amp;c. Animam sentiatis, oportet. Et si Christum &amp; Ecclesiam dixero, idem est, nisi quod Ecclesiæ nomine non vna Anima, sed multarum vnitas, vel potius vnanimitas designatur. S. Bern. serm. 61. in Cant.</p><p>Cant. 5. 5.</p><p>Cant. 1. 5.</p><p>Cant. 2. 2.</p><p>Cant. 4. 12.</p><p>Luk. 6. 26. Eccles. 15. 9.</p>",
