      {
        "day_number": 14,
        "title": "The Praise of a Godly Woman (Part 7) (2)",
        "content": "<p>Thus she was Angrie with her selfe for being Angrie with others, and then (according to Saint Ambroses rule) it ceased towards them before the Sunne went down ; and was not this Holy Reuenge on her selfe a true fruit of Euangelicall Repentance? 2 Cor. 7. 11.</p><p>But aye me! me thinkes I now heare her groaning vunder the dolefull pangs of Death, under those pangs of which she had foretold saying: I shall suffer much more before I goe hence. And can any have the heart to heare her groaning pangs, without renting his owne heart from his darling pleasure? without lamenting his owne sinnes, which vnlesse he forsake betimes, will bring him to euerlasting Burnings ? or without learning to compassionate every weake one, to assist any one yeelding vp the Ghost, because (as Saint Ambrose giues the reason ) the holy Peophet Iob desired the Blessing of one, that lyes a Dying: Benedictio morituri, in me veniat: Let the blessing of him that is readie to die, come upon me!</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Vitemus ergò aut temperemus iracūdiam: ne sit eius aut in Laudibus exceptio, aut in vitijs exaggeratio. S. Ambr. Off. lib. 1. cap. 21.</p><p>Iam. 1. 20. Psalm. 4. 4.</p><p>Ephes. 4. 26. Vel certè sic: Si irascimini, vobis irascimini, quia commoti estis, &amp; non peccabitis. Qui enim sibi irascitur, quia citò cōmotus est, desinit irasci alteri. Id. ibid.</p><p>Ezek. 18. 13, 30.</p><p>—Si quem viderimus pauperē moriturum, sumptu iuvemus, &amp; dicat vnusquisq, nostrûm: Benedictio morituri in me veniat: Si quē viderimus debilem, non deseramus, si quem in extremis positum, non relinquam s. S. Ambros. de Bono Mort. cap. 8. tom. 4.</p><p>Let vs then, not yet, leaue this Departing Saint. For in the midst of this her Agonie, after she had layen groaning many houres without any articulate or distinct speech, yet upon triall made of Her sense and memory by demanding of Her, whether she would have prayers made for Her, she answered plainly: With all my heart, pray, pray. And then as Gregory Nazianzen reports of his Father, that though He was daily, yes hourely, in great paine before his Death, yet He was ever still and quiet from paine, onely while Diuine Seruice was saying; so this Deuout Lady forgetting (as it were) Her former Groanings, did listen attentiuely to the prayers that were made for Her, without fetching so much as one sob during that time. And afterwards rehearsing distinctly part of the Lords Prayer, you might heare Her, when S. Stephens Vision and last words were read to Her, repeat very often these last words of Her Sauiour : O Heauenly Father into your hands I commend my spirit . Thus on a sudden I have told you how she Dyed in the Lord , and is with Christ .</p><p>And though we saw her afterwards mouing her lips; but heard not her Voice, no more than they that were at Saint Ambroses Death did heare his Voice; but only saw his lips moue ; yet we must not thinke The Spirit of strength , of Prayer and Grace , is then onely strong, when we heare a Dying Saint pray, because Christs Spirit cries in Gods Children, Abba, Father , with Vnutterable Groanings which we cannot heare, and therefore I doubt not, but this Elect Lady cried loudest in Gods eares, when we heard not her words; and why may we not thinke now, our sinnes hindered vs from hearing them? I am sure, previously she has spoken againe and againe many Heart-piercing speeches to deterre from Sinne, and to allure to Holinesse of Life. If she be not hearkened to now, from now on we shall heare Her speake no more. I charge you therefore before God and the Lord Jesus Christ, and the Elect Angells : you (I say) I charge whomsoeuer she has iustly reproued for any Sin, that you forsake those Sins; and whomsoeuer she has zealously exhorted to holy Duties, that you performe those holy Duties, for from now on you shall heare Her speake no more.</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Orat. 28. de Funere Patris. Tom. 2.</p><p>Act. 7. 53, 56, 59.</p><p>Luke 23. 46.</p><p>Reuel. 14. 13.</p><p>Phil. 1. 23.</p><p>Paulin. in D. Ambros Vita.</p><p>Esai. 11. 2.</p><p>Zach. 12. 10.</p><p>But why should I detaine you (Honourable Lady) any longer? Or what doe you (Beloued) expect more? That our speech also should Die together with Hers, and (as it were) be Buried together with Her. O my Blessed Brethren, suffer neither this Godly Lady, nor Gods word to depart so dishonourably from you.</p><p>I. Imitation of Her Vertues.</p><p>Suffer not Her so to depart from you; but let her ever liue in your breasts by Esteeming Her very highly in love for Her workes sake, by Commemorating Gods Graces in Her; but aboue all by Imitating her Faith and Vertues . Then you shall not need to grieue very much for Her Absence from you; because she is with Christ, which is best of all ; because she is Taken away , not from you; but from the evil to come, Esai. 57. 1. From you she is not Taken, but from seeing the plagues and miseries of this wretched world, yes from seeing the future Deaths of you, Her Dearest friends , for whom she would have wept full bitterly; but you have greater Cause, if you will heare S. Ambrose and S. Ierome comforting themselues in a like case, to Reioyce and to give God thankes , that you Have had Her, no that you Have Her, if so be you follow Her good example, and represent her to the life by your Godly Life.</p><p>FOOTNOTES:</p><p>Gal. 4. 6.</p><p>Rom. 8. 26.</p><p>1 Tim. 5. 21.</p>",
